module

public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.FormalGeometry.DerivedCompletionRinged.InverseImageIdealSheaf
public import StacksFrontier.Algebra.Category.ModuleCat.Sheaf.RingedToposUnitStructureMap
public import StacksFrontier.Algebra.Category.ModuleCat.Sheaf.RingedToposPullbackUnitValue
public import StacksFrontier.Algebra.Category.ModuleCat.Sheaf.ImageSubsheafUniversal

/-!
# The ideal sheaf `ℐ' = f^{-1}ℐ · 𝒪_X`, for a morphism of ringed topoi

Section 52.7 of the source, and with it [Stacks, Tag 0A0G], is stated for a morphism of ringed
**topoi** `f : (Sh(𝒟), 𝒪') ⟶ (Sh(𝒞), 𝒪)` and builds out of a sheaf of ideals `ℐ ⊂ 𝒪` the sheaf of
ideals `ℐ' = f^{-1}ℐ · 𝒪_X ⊂ 𝒪'`. `InverseImageIdealSheaf.lean` builds it for a morphism of
ringed **sites**, where the construction can be made from the functor `u` presenting the
morphism. This file builds it for an arbitrary `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedToposHom` — a geometric
morphism of the underlying topoi together with `f^♯` — where there is no such functor.

## The construction

The inverse image of sheaves of modules along a morphism of ringed topoi is
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedToposHom.pullbackModules`, the inverse image of sheaves of abelian
groups followed by extension of scalars along `f^♯`. At the unit sheaf of modules it is
canonically `𝒪'`: `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedToposHom.pullbackUnitStructureMap` is the comparison
`f^*𝒪 ⟶ 𝒪'`, the transpose of the unit-module structure map `𝒪 ⟶ f_*𝒪'` under
[Stacks, Tag 03D7], and it is invertible with no hypothesis on `f` beyond the standing
sheafification instances by `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedToposHom.isIso_pullbackUnitStructureMap` —
in contrast to the ringed-sites case, where the corresponding comparison
`SheafOfModules.pullbackObjUnitToUnit` has to be shown invertible by hand. So

`inverseImageIdealMap f ℐ : f^*ℐ ⟶ 𝒪'`

is `f^*` of the inclusion `ℐ ↪ 𝒪` followed by that comparison, and

`inverseImageIdealSheaf f ℐ`

is its image subsheaf, `Stacks.SheafOfModules.ImageSubsheaf.imageSubmodule`, which is defined
over any site with no further hypothesis. Multiplication by `𝒪'` is what the extension of scalars
inside `f^*` performs: `f^*ℐ` is `f^{-1}ℐ ⊗_{f^{-1}𝒪} 𝒪'`, so the image of `f^*ℐ ⟶ 𝒪'` is the
`𝒪'`-submodule generated by `f^♯(f^{-1}ℐ)`, which is what `f^{-1}ℐ · 𝒪_X` denotes.

## The universal property

`inverseImageIdealSheaf_le_iff` says a sheaf of ideals `𝒥 ⊂ 𝒪'` contains `ℐ'` exactly when
`f^*ℐ ⟶ 𝒪'` factors through `𝒥`, and `inverseImageIdealSheaf_le_iff_forall_mem` says the same
with the factorisation replaced by the objectwise membership it is equivalent to. Together with
`idealMap_factorThru_ι`, which is the factorisation through `ℐ'` itself, this characterises `ℐ'`
as the least sheaf of ideals of `𝒪'` receiving `f^*ℐ`, so it agrees with every other
construction — in particular this is the exact analogue of the ringed-sites
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.inverseImageIdealSheaf_le_iff`, whose right-hand side is
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.PushforwardIdealCompatible`, the sections form that a presenting
functor `u` makes available.

*Why the universal property is stated as a factorisation and not as a condition on sections.*
At a morphism of ringed sites a section of `f_*𝒥` over `W` is a section of `𝒥` over `u(W)`, so
the adjunct condition is objectwise and `PushforwardIdealCompatible` can be written down. At a
morphism of ringed topoi there is no `u`, the direct image is not computed on objects, and the
sections of `f^*ℐ` are not presented by anything either; the factorisation is what remains, and
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.ImageSubsheaf.exists_factorThru_iff_imageSubmodule_le` is what makes it
equivalent to containment.

## What the two properties of the construction are, and where

Both things [Stacks, Tag 0A0G] needs of this construction at the level of ringed topoi are proved,
each in its own module importing this one.

* `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedToposHom.IdealCompatible f ℐ (inverseImageIdealSheaf f ℐ)`, the
  relation of [Stacks, Tag 099J] between the two sheaves of ideals, which the topos-level
  comparison map of `PushforwardCommutesCompletionTopoi.lean` requires in order to exist. That
  relation is a factorisation of `f^{-1}ℐ ⟶ f^{-1}𝒪 ⟶ 𝒪'` in sheaves of **sets**, while what is
  available here is a factorisation in sheaves of **modules**.
  `InverseImageIdealSheafToposCompatible.lean` proves the relation in its **direct-image** form,
  `(ℐ ↪ 𝒪) ≫ f^♯ = ψ ≫ f_*(ℐ' ↪ 𝒪')`, which is the form the consumers of `IdealCompatible`
  actually work with, and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedToposHom.idealCompatible_inverseImageIdealSheaf` of
  `InverseImageIdealSheafToposIdealCompatible.lean` transposes that back along `f^{-1} ⊣ f_*` to
  `IdealCompatible` itself. No hypothesis on `ℐ` and none on `f`.
* Finite type of `inverseImageIdealSheaf f ℐ` for `ℐ` of finite type:
  `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedToposHom.isFiniteType_inverseImageIdealSheaf` of
  `InverseImageIdealSheafToposFiniteType.lean`, the counterpart of the ringed-sites
  `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isFiniteType_inverseImageIdealSheaf`, by the same two steps.

In particular, preservation of finite type by `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedToposHom.pullbackModules`
at a general morphism of ringed topoi is available:
`Stacks.Sheaves.FiniteType.pullbackModules_isFiniteType` is [Stacks, Tag 03DO] for the modules
development's pullback at an **arbitrary** four-field morphism, and `pullbackModules` here is that
functor at `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedToposHom.toModulesHom` by definition, so it transfers on the
nose. By contrast,
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedToposHom.isFiniteType_iff_pullbackModules` asks `f` to be an equivalence —
`f.toToposHom.pushforward.IsEquivalence` and `IsIso f.fSharp` — so it gives invariance of finite
type under the dense-subsite replacement of [Stacks, Tag 03CR], not preservation along an
arbitrary `f`.

## Deliberately untagged

This is the API of a construction. Section 52.7 introduces `f^{-1}ℐ · 𝒪_X` in passing rather than
as a numbered statement, and the ringed-sites counterpart in `InverseImageIdealSheaf.lean`
carries no attribute either.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 03D6](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/03D6)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 099J](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/099J)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0A0G](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0A0G)
-/

@[expose] public noncomputable section

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits Opposite

universe u

namespace AlgebraicGeometry.RingedToposHom

variable {X Y : RingedTopos.{u, u, u}}
  [X.J.HasSheafCompose (forget RingCat.{u})] [Y.J.HasSheafCompose (forget RingCat.{u})]
  [X.J.HasSheafCompose (forget₂ CommRingCat.{u} RingCat.{u})]
  [Y.J.HasSheafCompose (forget₂ CommRingCat.{u} RingCat.{u})]
  [X.J.HasSheafCompose (forget AddCommGrpCat.{u})]
  [Y.J.HasSheafCompose (forget AddCommGrpCat.{u})]
  [HasWeakSheafify X.J AddCommGrpCat.{u}] [X.J.WEqualsLocallyBijective AddCommGrpCat.{u}]
  (f : RingedToposHom X Y)
  (I : SheafOfModules.Submodule (SheafOfModules.unit (Stacks.SheafOfModules.Tensor.ringSheaf Y.O)))

/-- **The canonical map `f^*ℐ ⟶ 𝒪'`, for a morphism of ringed topoi**: the inverse image of the
inclusion `ℐ ↪ 𝒪`, followed by the canonical comparison `f^*𝒪 ⟶ 𝒪'` of
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedToposHom.pullbackUnitStructureMap`.

Unlike the ringed-sites `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.inverseImageIdealMap`, whose second factor
`SheafOfModules.pullbackObjUnitToUnit` requires a proof of invertibility, here that comparison is
invertible outright, by `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedToposHom.isIso_pullbackUnitStructureMap`.

The second factor is spelled as `pullbackUnitStructureMap` rather than as the inverse of
`Stacks.Sheaves.RingedToposModules.pullbackModulesObjUnitIso` — the same morphism, by
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedToposHom.pullbackModulesObjUnitIso_hom_comp_pullbackUnitStructureMap` —
because it is the spelling that lives over this development's own `pullbackModules` and
`pullbackAdjPushforwardModules` rather than over the modules development's four-field morphism,
and so is the one whose transpose is available on the nose. -/
def inverseImageIdealMap :
    ((f.toModulesHom.pullbackModules).obj I.toSheafOfModules) ⟶
      SheafOfModules.unit (Stacks.SheafOfModules.Tensor.ringSheaf X.O) :=
  (f.toModulesHom.pullbackModules).map I.ι ≫ pullbackUnitStructureMap f

/-- **The ideal sheaf `ℐ' = f^{-1}ℐ · 𝒪_X` of Section 52.7, for an arbitrary morphism of ringed
topoi**: the image subsheaf of the canonical map `f^*ℐ ⟶ 𝒪'`.

The multiplication by `𝒪'` that the notation `f^{-1}ℐ · 𝒪_X` denotes is performed by the
extension of scalars inside `f^*`: `f^*ℐ = f^{-1}ℐ ⊗_{f^{-1}𝒪} 𝒪'`, so the image of
`f^*ℐ ⟶ 𝒪'` is the `𝒪'`-submodule generated by `f^♯(f^{-1}ℐ)`.

Being an image it is the least sheaf of ideals of `𝒪'` through which that map factors, by
`inverseImageIdealSheaf_le_iff`; that is the defining property Section 52.7 asks of
`f^{-1}ℐ · 𝒪_X`, and the ringed-sites construction
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.inverseImageIdealSheaf` satisfies it in the same form. -/
def inverseImageIdealSheaf :
    SheafOfModules.Submodule (SheafOfModules.unit (Stacks.SheafOfModules.Tensor.ringSheaf X.O)) :=
  Stacks.SheafOfModules.ImageSubsheaf.imageSubmodule (inverseImageIdealMap f I)

/-- **The canonical map `f^*ℐ ⟶ 𝒪'` factors through `ℐ' = f^{-1}ℐ · 𝒪_X`**, by construction: `ℐ'`
is its image subsheaf, and a map factors through its own image.

This is one half of the defining property of `ℐ'`; `inverseImageIdealSheaf_le_iff` is the
other. -/
def idealMapFactorThru :
    ((f.toModulesHom.pullbackModules).obj I.toSheafOfModules) ⟶
      (inverseImageIdealSheaf f I).toSheafOfModules :=
  (inverseImageIdealSheaf f I).factorThru (inverseImageIdealMap f I)
    fun _ m ↦ Stacks.SheafOfModules.ImageSubsheaf.apply_mem_imageSubmodule
      (inverseImageIdealMap f I) m

/-- The factorisation `idealMapFactorThru f I` of `f^*ℐ ⟶ 𝒪'` through `ℐ'`, followed by the
inclusion `ℐ' ⟶ 𝒪'`, is the canonical map `inverseImageIdealMap f I`. -/
@[simp]
theorem idealMapFactorThru_ι :
    idealMapFactorThru f I ≫ (inverseImageIdealSheaf f I).ι = inverseImageIdealMap f I :=
  (inverseImageIdealSheaf f I).factorThru_ι (inverseImageIdealMap f I) _

section Minimality

variable (Q : SheafOfModules.Submodule
    (SheafOfModules.unit (Stacks.SheafOfModules.Tensor.ringSheaf X.O)))

/-- **`ℐ' = f^{-1}ℐ · 𝒪_X` is the least sheaf of ideals of `𝒪'` through which `f^*ℐ ⟶ 𝒪'`
factors**, for an arbitrary morphism of ringed topoi: a sheaf of ideals `𝒥 ⊂ 𝒪'` contains `ℐ'`
exactly when that map factors through `𝒥`.

Together with `idealMapFactorThru_ι` this is the defining property Section 52.7 asks of
`f^{-1}ℐ · 𝒪_X`, and it is what identifies the construction with every other one. It is the
topos-level form of `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.inverseImageIdealSheaf_le_iff`, whose
right-hand side is the sections condition
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.PushforwardIdealCompatible` that a presenting functor `u` makes
available and that a morphism of ringed topoi does not. -/
theorem inverseImageIdealSheaf_le_iff :
    inverseImageIdealSheaf f I ≤ Q ↔
      ∃ φ : ((f.toModulesHom.pullbackModules).obj I.toSheafOfModules) ⟶ Q.toSheafOfModules,
        φ ≫ Q.ι = inverseImageIdealMap f I :=
  (Stacks.SheafOfModules.ImageSubsheaf.exists_factorThru_iff_imageSubmodule_le
    (inverseImageIdealMap f I)).symm

/-- The universal property of `ℐ' = f^{-1}ℐ · 𝒪_X` in objectwise form: a sheaf of ideals of `𝒪'`
contains it exactly when it contains every value of `f^*ℐ ⟶ 𝒪'`.

The hypothesis is checked one object at a time and the conclusion is local, membership in a
sheaf of ideals being a local condition; this is
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.ImageSubsheaf.imageSubmodule_le_iff`. -/
theorem inverseImageIdealSheaf_le_iff_forall_mem :
    inverseImageIdealSheaf f I ≤ Q ↔
      ∀ (U : X.Cᵒᵖ) (m : ((f.toModulesHom.pullbackModules).obj I.toSheafOfModules).val.obj U),
        (inverseImageIdealMap f I).val.app U m ∈ Q.obj U :=
  Stacks.SheafOfModules.ImageSubsheaf.imageSubmodule_le_iff (inverseImageIdealMap f I)

end Minimality

end AlgebraicGeometry.RingedToposHom

end
